//@version=6
// MORAI · Expected Move — how far the index is priced to travel today, and how much of that
// is still ahead of you.
//
// ALL CLOCK TIMES IN THIS FILE ARE CENTRAL, matching the study's default session
// (0830-1500 America/Chicago). The SPX cash session is 0930-1600 Eastern; same session,
// one hour earlier on the clock.
//
// The question this answers is the one you actually ask at 08:45: "it has already moved a
// third of a percent — how much is left?" Every public expected-move script on TradingView
// draws the FIXED band and stops there. A fixed band answers "how far by the close", which
// stops being the live question about four minutes into the session.
//
// So there are two numbers here, and they are not the same number:
//   · DAY   — the full-session envelope, anchored once and never moved.
//   · LEFT  — the remaining move from CURRENT spot, shrunk by the variance already spent.
// LEFT is the hero. DAY is the context it sits inside.
//
// THE √t LAW. Vol scales with the square root of time, never with time. Half the session gone
// does NOT leave half the move — it leaves √0.5 = 71% of it. Subtracting a realised move from
// the daily expected move ("1.00% expected, 0.33% done, so 0.67% left") is the single most
// common way to get this wrong, and it under-states the remaining move every time.
//
// ANCHOR AND SIGMA MUST MATCH. There are three ways to pair them and only two are right:
//   · anchor = prior RTH close + FULL-day sigma            -> consistent
//   · anchor = today's open    + sigma x sqrt(RTH share)   -> consistent
//   · anchor = today's open    + FULL-day sigma            -> WRONG, band too wide
// The third pairing is what most published scripts do, and it is why their bands look
// generous. Here the anchor choice SELECTS the sigma; they cannot be set independently.
//
// SOURCE. CBOE:VIX1D is the horizon-matched input — it is the same variance-swap methodology
// as VIX applied to 0DTE/1DTE SPXW options, so it measures the CURRENT session rather than the
// next 30 days. VIX is offered as the fallback everyone knows, and it is a proxy: a 30-day
// number spread evenly over a single day, which runs rich on a quiet session and cheap into an
// event. The table always names which one is feeding it, because a 1-day implied and a 30-day
// proxy deserve different amounts of trust.
//
// VIX1D HAS A KNOWN ARTEFACT. Albers & Kestner (J. Behavioral & Experimental Finance, 2024)
// document a mechanical overnight bias: VIX1D's dynamic weighting of the 1DTE leg makes it
// drift UP through the session and gap DOWN overnight, independent of any real information.
// Seen live on this account 2026-08-21 pre-open: VIX1D 12.31, +26.1% on the day while VIX was
// -2.1%. That is the artefact, not a vol event. Practical consequence: read VIX1D near the
// open, and treat a rising VIX1D late in the session as partly mechanical.
//
// WHICH VIX1D THE BAND ACTUALLY USES, and why that is the right one. The request is
// `close[1]` with lookahead_on — the documented non-repainting idiom — so through session T the
// study reads VIX1D's close from session T-1 on EVERY bar, history and live alike.
//
// It did not always. The first version used a bare `close` with lookahead_off, which is correct
// on historical bars but hands back the still-FORMING daily bar on the realtime one. Caught live
// 2026-08-21 13:38 CT: the panel was building the band from that day's live VIX1D 9.86 instead
// of the 12.31 it closed at on the 20th — a band 20% narrower than designed, drifting all
// session, and contaminated by exactly the intraday artefact described above. It also silently
// broke the match with the backtest, which assumes the prior close. `close[1]` + lookahead_on
// fixes all three. Verified on this chart: through 2026-08-20 the band was built from
// VIX1D 9.76 (the Aug 19 close), giving sigma 0.6148% against 0.6147% measured off the plotted
// levels. That is not staleness. VIX1D's close on day T is the market's price for day T+1, so
// the last COMPLETED close is exactly the forward-looking number for the session you are in —
// and taking it that way sidesteps the intraday drift artefact described above, because the
// contaminated part of VIX1D's path never reaches the script. The cost is that a vol event
// which erupts after today's open cannot widen today's band. Watch VIX1D on the watchlist for
// that; the band is what was PRICED for today, not a live re-read of it.
//
// CALIBRATION IS MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. tools/tradingview/backtest-expected-move.ts runs this
// exact band against CBOE's own published history and reports coverage against the 68.27% a
// 1-sigma band owes you. Headline on 1,070 sessions: VIX1D 72.6%, VIX9D 77.9%, VIX 80.1% — so
// the band is too WIDE, and the 30-day index is ~12pp too generous over a single session. That
// is the empirical case for the Auto mapping above, and the standing caveat on VXN/RVX/VXD:
// they are 30-day indices, so on QQQ/IWM/DIA expect the same over-generosity.
//
// The error is NOT a constant scale factor — it shrinks as vol rises. Tagging each session by
// the VIX level AT BAND-SET TIME (the prior close), the fitted haircut runs 1.13 / 1.11 / 1.08
// across VIX terciles for VIX1D and 1.57 / 1.33 / 1.15 for the 30-day VIX. Every one of those
// is above 1.00, so the band is too wide in EVERY regime, just least so in stress. One divisor
// therefore cannot fit all three, and the 30-day index cannot be rescued by any single number.
//
// VVIX IS DELIBERATELY NOT AN INPUT, AND NOT A FLAG EITHER. Vol-of-vol does not price SPX's
// move, so it must not change the band's WIDTH — that much was always the design. What this
// header used to claim in addition, that a high VVIX raises the 2-sigma breach rate inside the
// same VIX bucket and so earns a place as a "trust this width less today" panel flag, is
// REFUTED. That finding was produced by tagging each session with VVIX read at the close of the
// day being predicted, which conditions on the answer: a big move raises VVIX that same day.
// Re-measured with VVIX at the prior close — the only value a non-repainting study can read —
// the effect is gone. Stress-tercile 2-sigma breach 2.95% (low VVIX) vs 3.07% (high VVIX) on
// n = 1,696, and mean|z| within VIX deciles gives t = +1.18 on n = 5,088; same-day tagging on
// the identical days gives 3.07% vs 5.18%. MORAI's regime board had already deferred VVIX/VIX
// as double-counting VIX, and the board was right. See backtest-expected-move.ts section 3b,
// which now prints both taggings side by side.
//
// SYMBOLS ARE PROBE-VERIFIED ON THIS ACCOUNT (2026-07-31, real values through
// request.security(), not symbol search): CBOE:VIX 17.09 · CBOE:VIX1D 14.14 · CBOE:VIX9D 14.85.
// tools/tradingview/README.md carries the law that a ticker resolving in symbol search does
// NOT mean it resolves in request.security().
//
// ONE VOL INDEX PER UNDERLYING, PICKED FROM THE CHART. VIX measures SPX and nothing else, so
// pointing it at QQQ prices Nasdaq risk with S&P vol — measured on this chart 2026-08-21, that
// printed a QQQ band of 705.44-716.46 at SPX's 0.78% while reading a calm "97% used". A
// confidently wrong band is worse than no band. So `Auto` maps the chart to its own index:
//     SPX · SPX500 · SPY · ES  ->  VIX1D      (a real 1-day measurement)
//     NDX · NAS100 · QQQ · NQ  ->  VXN        (30-day proxy — there is no VXN1D)
//     RUT · IWM · RTY          ->  RVX        (30-day proxy)
//     DJI · DIA · YM           ->  VXD        (30-day proxy)
// Anything else refuses: no bands, and the SRC row names the symbol it could not map. Only the
// SPX family gets a true one-session number; CBOE never built 1-day versions of the others, and
// the table says "30-day proxy" out loud on those so the reading is never mistaken for one.
//
// A futures root is only honoured when syminfo.type is actually "futures" — otherwise
// str.startswith(ticker, "ES") would quietly hand Essex Property Trust the S&P's volatility.
//
// WHY SIX FETCHES AND A TERNARY ON THE FLOAT. Every symbol argument below is a literal const
// string. The source dropdown then picks between the RESULTING FLOATS. Building the ticker
// instead ("CBOE:" + choice) would work, but v6 turns any non-const symbol argument into a
// dynamic request, and a dynamic request that was never exercised on a historical bar throws
// at runtime on the first realtime bar — which is exactly the failure this repo has already
// paid for: the study dies ONLY on-chart while the Pine editor reports zero errors.
// `dynamic_requests = false` on the declaration below turns that silent runtime death into a
// hard compile error, permanently. Five extra request.security() calls is a cheap fee for it.
indicator("MORAI · Expected Move", overlay = true, dynamic_requests = false,
     max_labels_count = 20, max_lines_count = 200, max_boxes_count = 50)

// ─── Inputs ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
string src = input.string("Auto", "Implied vol source",
     options = ["Auto", "VIX1D", "VIX9D", "VIX", "VXN", "RVX", "VXD"], group = "Source",
     tooltip = "Auto reads the chart and picks the index that measures IT — see the mapping " +
               "table in the source. Override only if you know why: naming an index that does " +
               "not match the chart prices one underlying with another's risk, and the SRC " +
               "row will call that out rather than hide it. VIX1D measures TODAY; VIX9D is " +
               "the ~2-week read; VIX/VXN/RVX/VXD are 30-day, so on a single session they are " +
               "a proxy, not a measurement.")

float haircut = input.float(1.0, "VRP haircut divisor", minval = 1.0, maxval = 1.5, step = 0.05,
     group = "Source",
     tooltip = "Implied vol prices ABOVE what the index realises. MEASURED on this exact " +
               "construction (tools/tradingview/backtest-expected-move.ts, 1,070 sessions " +
               "2022-05 to 2026-08): 1.107 for VIX1D, which lands 1-sigma coverage on 68.0% " +
               "against the 68.27% target. NOT the 1.15-1.25 the literature quotes — that " +
               "figure is for 30-day VIX, and 30-day VIX on this construction measures 1.31. " +
               "WARNING: one divisor cannot fit every regime. Splitting by the VIX level at " +
               "BAND-SET TIME, the fitted haircut is 1.13 / 1.11 / 1.08 across VIX terciles " +
               "for VIX1D and 1.57 / 1.33 / 1.15 for VIX — so a divisor tuned on the pooled " +
               "sample runs too aggressive in stress. It does not flip sign: every tercile " +
               "measures above 1.00, so the band is never too narrow on average. An earlier " +
               "version of this tooltip claimed a 2%-to-5% breach ramp across terciles; that " +
               "came from tagging each day with its OWN closing VIX and does not survive " +
               "prior-close tagging (3.66 / 2.79 / 3.07%, flat). Leave it at 1.00 for the " +
               "honest risk-neutral number, which is what you want for anything " +
               "hedging-related. Every figure above is hand-copied from " +
               "backtest-expected-move.md, which the script REGENERATES on each run — that " +
               "file is the source of truth, and it wins any disagreement with this tooltip.")

string anchorMode = input.string("Prior RTH close", "Anchor the band on",
     options = ["Prior RTH close", "Today's open"], group = "Band",
     tooltip = "This also selects the sigma — see the ANCHOR AND SIGMA MUST MATCH note in the " +
               "source. Anchoring on the open uses only the RTH slice of the day's variance, " +
               "because the overnight slice is already spent by the time the bell rings.")

float onShare = input.float(0.22, "Overnight share of daily variance", minval = 0.0,
     maxval = 0.60, step = 0.01, group = "Band",
     tooltip = "Close-to-open variance as a fraction of close-to-close. The literature " +
               "converges on 15-30% for SPX (French & Roll 1986 onwards) rather than one " +
               "canonical figure, so 0.22 is the middle of that range and NOT a measured " +
               "constant. Compute it from your own history and pin it if it matters.")

string decay = input.string("U-shape", "Intraday variance profile", options = ["U-shape", "Linear"],
     group = "Remaining",
     tooltip = "Linear spends variance evenly across the session. It is wrong — intraday " +
               "volatility is U-shaped (Wood/McInish/Ord 1985, Andersen & Bollerslev 1997): " +
               "heavy at the open, light at midday, heavy into the close. Linear therefore " +
               "OVER-states what is left at midday and UNDER-states it near the bell.")

float uRatio = input.float(2.5, "U-shape open/close vs midday ratio", minval = 1.0, maxval = 5.0,
     step = 0.1, group = "Remaining",
     tooltip = "How much hotter the open and close are than the midday trough. 1.0 collapses " +
               "the curve back to Linear. Calibration knob, not a law.")

// 0830-1500 CENTRAL is the same session as 0930-1600 Eastern, to the minute — the SPX cash
// session expressed on a Chicago clock. Nothing downstream changes: every timestamp in this
// script is built with the tz below, so the spec and the zone move together or not at all.
string rthSpec = input.session("0830-1500", "RTH session", group = "Remaining",
     tooltip = "Drives the elapsed-fraction, so it must be the CASH session even when the " +
               "chart is a 24h CFD or futures proxy — and it must be written on the clock " +
               "named in Session timezone below. The pair 0830-1500 + America/Chicago and " +
               "the pair 0930-1600 + America/New_York are the SAME session; mixing one half " +
               "of each is the way to get a band that is an hour out. On a half day set this " +
               "to 0830-1200 (1200 Central = 1300 Eastern) — in 2026 that is Nov 27 and " +
               "Dec 24. The spec cannot know that for you.")

// US ZONES ONLY, and that is not laziness. This input exists to say which clock rthSpec is
// written on, not to display anything in local time, so the only useful values are zones where
// the US cash session sits at a FIXED wall-clock time. All four below share the US DST
// calendar, so 0830-1500 Central is the same session in March as in July. London or UTC would
// not be: the UK and the US switch on different dates, so for two or three weeks each spring
// and autumn the session would silently slide by an hour.
string tz = input.string("America/Chicago", "Session timezone", group = "Remaining",
     options = ["America/Chicago", "America/New_York", "America/Denver",
                "America/Los_Angeles"],
     tooltip = "The clock the RTH session above is written on. Change this and you must " +
               "rewrite the session to match — Central 0830-1500, Eastern 0930-1600, " +
               "Mountain 0730-1400, Pacific 0630-1300.")

bool show2sd  = input.bool(true, "Draw the 2σ envelope", group = "Display")

bool showZone = input.bool(true, "Shade the 1σ zone", group = "Display",
     tooltip = "The tint between +1σ and −1σ. Turning it off keeps every level and removes " +
               "only the fill — same information, far less ink, which is what you want once " +
               "more than one session is on the chart.")

int histSess = input.int(1, "Sessions drawn", minval = 1, maxval = 30, group = "Display",
     tooltip = "How many RTH sessions of bands to keep, counting back from the most recent. " +
               "1 is today only. 5 is a trading week. Counted in SESSIONS and not calendar " +
               "days, so a holiday or a half day cannot quietly shift the window. Prior " +
               "sessions draw dimmer than the live one, so the band you are actually trading " +
               "stays the loud one.")

bool showTbl = input.bool(true, "Show readout panel", group = "Display")

// ─── Fetch ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `close[1]` + lookahead_on, deliberately, and the pairing matters. lookahead_on ALONE would
// leak the future into history and is the thing the README warns about; the [1] offset is what
// makes it safe, because it asks for a bar that had already closed. The result is the same
// number on history and live — which plain lookahead_off does NOT give you, since it serves the
// forming daily bar once the chart goes realtime.
iv(string sym) =>
    request.security(sym, "D", close[1], ignore_invalid_symbol = true,
         lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)

float v1d = iv("CBOE:VIX1D")
float v9d = iv("CBOE:VIX9D")
float v30 = iv("CBOE:VIX")
float vxn = iv("CBOE:VXN")
float rvx = iv("CBOE:RVX")
float vxd = iv("CBOE:VXD")

// Which index family the CHART belongs to. Exact matches for the three-letter ETFs on purpose:
// str.contains(u, "DIA") also matches INDIA.
famOf(string t, string ty) =>
    string u = str.upper(t)
    bool fut = ty == "futures"
    string f = "?"
    if str.contains(u, "SPX") or str.contains(u, "SP500") or str.contains(u, "US500")
         or u == "SPY" or (fut and str.startswith(u, "ES"))
        f := "SPX"
    else if str.contains(u, "NDX") or str.contains(u, "NAS100") or str.contains(u, "US100")
         or u == "QQQ" or (fut and str.startswith(u, "NQ"))
        f := "NDX"
    else if str.contains(u, "RUT") or str.contains(u, "US2000") or u == "IWM"
         or (fut and str.startswith(u, "RTY"))
        f := "RUT"
    else if str.contains(u, "DJI") or str.contains(u, "US30") or u == "DIA"
         or (fut and str.startswith(u, "YM"))
        f := "DJI"
    f

string fam = famOf(syminfo.ticker, syminfo.type)

// SPX is the only family CBOE built a 1-day index for; the rest fall back to their 30-day.
string autoSrc = fam == "SPX" ? "VIX1D" : fam == "NDX" ? "VXN"
     : fam == "RUT" ? "RVX" : fam == "DJI" ? "VXD" : "NONE"
string useSrc = src == "Auto" ? autoSrc : src

float idx = useSrc == "VIX1D" ? v1d : useSrc == "VIX9D" ? v9d : useSrc == "VIX" ? v30
     : useSrc == "VXN" ? vxn : useSrc == "RVX" ? rvx : useSrc == "VXD" ? vxd : na

// A manual override pointed at the wrong underlying still computes — the user asked for it —
// but it is never allowed to look like a match.
string srcFam = useSrc == "VXN" ? "NDX" : useSrc == "RVX" ? "RUT" : useSrc == "VXD" ? "DJI"
     : useSrc == "NONE" ? "?" : "SPX"
bool mismatch = fam != "?" and srcFam != "?" and fam != srcFam

// ─── Session bookkeeping ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Built from an explicit session spec rather than session.isfirstbar_regular, because the
// chart this runs on is usually a 24h proxy (CAPITALCOM:SPX500, ES1!) whose "regular session"
// is not the SPX cash session at all. The spec is symbol-independent.
int  rthNow = time(timeframe.period, rthSpec, tz)
bool inRth  = not na(rthNow)
bool rthOpenBar = inRth and not inRth[1]

var float priorClose = na   // last RTH close of the PREVIOUS session
var float lastClose  = na   // running last RTH close of the CURRENT session
var float dayOpen    = na

if inRth
    if rthOpenBar
        priorClose := lastClose      // read BEFORE lastClose is overwritten below
        dayOpen    := open
    lastClose := close

// SESSION END, parsed from the spec. The obvious-looking
//     time_close(timeframe.period, rthSpec, tz)
// is a TRAP and cost a live session to find: it returns the CURRENT BAR's close time, not the
// session's end. The elapsed fraction then computed (time_close - start)/(time_close - start)
// = exactly 1.0 on every RTH bar, so the panel read "100% elapsed · 0% var left" from the
// opening bell and LEFT — the whole point of this study — printed ±0.00 all day. It never
// surfaced pre-market because outside RTH the row correctly says "pre/post".
//
// Parsing "0830-1500" is the honest fix and it is what makes the half-day edit actually work:
// change the input to 0830-1200 and the denominator really does shrink. The offsets are fixed
// because the spec is fixed-width HHMM-HHMM, so they hold on any clock; substring(5, 9) also
// survives a day-mask suffix like "0830-1500:23456".
int shh = int(math.round(str.tonumber(str.substring(rthSpec, 0, 2))))
int smm = int(math.round(str.tonumber(str.substring(rthSpec, 2, 4))))
int ehh = int(math.round(str.tonumber(str.substring(rthSpec, 5, 7))))
int emm = int(math.round(str.tonumber(str.substring(rthSpec, 7, 9))))

int yy = year(time_close, tz), mo = month(time_close, tz), dd = dayofmonth(time_close, tz)
int sessStart = timestamp(tz, yy, mo, dd, shh, smm)
int sessEnd   = timestamp(tz, yy, mo, dd, ehh, emm)

// WHICH SESSION IS THE LIVE ONE, knowable while HISTORICAL bars are still executing. That is
// the whole trick behind keeping plot() for today: `last_bar_time` is a constant on every bar,
// so the last bar's calendar date — and therefore the last session's start — is available
// everywhere, unlike "how many sessions remain after me", which is not.
int lastSessStart = timestamp(tz, year(last_bar_time, tz), month(last_bar_time, tz),
     dayofmonth(last_bar_time, tz), shh, smm)
bool isLastSess = sessStart == lastSessStart

// On history the bar's own close time is the honest "now". On the live bar it is up to one bar
// into the future, so use the wall clock — otherwise LEFT jumps a full bar ahead of itself.
int nowMs = barstate.isrealtime ? timenow : time_close

// Clamped, so a bad session spec degrades to a sane number instead of a negative square root.
float uRaw = inRth and sessEnd > sessStart ? (nowMs - sessStart) / (sessEnd - sessStart) : na
float u = na(uRaw) ? na : math.max(0.0, math.min(1.0, uRaw))

// ─── Variance profile ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The share of the session's variance still AHEAD at elapsed-fraction u.
//
// Linear treats variance density as flat: remaining = 1 - u. The U-shape uses the simplest
// density that reproduces the stylised fact, a parabola in u:
//     f(u) = c0 + c1*(2u-1)^2 ,  normalised so the integral over the session is 1
// with R = f(0)/f(0.5) = the open/close-to-midday ratio. Solving c0 + c1/3 = 1 and
// c1 = c0*(R-1) gives c0 = 3/(R+2), c1 = 3(R-1)/(R+2), and the cumulative has a closed form:
//     F(u) = c0*u + c1*((2u-1)^3 + 1)/6
// This is a shape, not a fit. Andersen & Bollerslev's Flexible Fourier Form (or an empirical
// per-bucket table built from your own trailing squared returns) is the rigorous version. The
// parabola gets the first-order effect for one input and no history.
remainShare(float uu) =>
    float r = na
    if not na(uu)
        if decay == "Linear" or uRatio <= 1.0
            r := 1.0 - uu
        else
            float c0 = 3.0 / (uRatio + 2.0)
            float c1 = 3.0 * (uRatio - 1.0) / (uRatio + 2.0)
            float z  = 2.0 * uu - 1.0
            r := 1.0 - (c0 * uu + c1 * (z * z * z + 1.0) / 6.0)
    na(r) ? na : math.max(0.0, math.min(1.0, r))

// ─── The move ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// VIX-family indices are annualised vol points. /100 to a fraction, /sqrt(252) to one session.
//
// FOR VIX1D THIS IS EXACT, NOT A CONVENTION. Verified against CBOE's own methodology PDF
// (Volatility_Index_Methodology_Cboe_1-Day_Volatility_Index.pdf): "The calculation of the VIX1D
// Index uses business years and business minutes during the Regular Trading Hours (RTH) session
// ... a different increment than the VIX Index, which uses calendar years and calendar minutes."
// It defines M_year = 252 x 6.75 x 60 = 102,060 business minutes and one full session as
// M = 405, so a session is 405/102,060 = EXACTLY 1/252. Dividing VIX1D by sqrt(252) therefore
// inverts the index's own definition precisely — weekends contribute ZERO business minutes.
//
// FOR VIX / VXN / RVX / VXD IT IS A CONVENTION, and a slightly inconsistent one: those ARE
// annualised on 365 calendar days. sqrt(252) is nonetheless the right de-annualiser because
// variance accrues only when the market is open — French & Roll (1986) measured a three-day
// weekend carrying just +10.7% more variance than one session. This is the "rule of 16"
// (sqrt(252) = 15.87), and verify-expected-move.ts shows sqrt(365) scoring better on raw
// coverage only by cancelling that error against the risk premium. Do not take the trade.
float sigDay = na(idx) ? na : (idx / 100.0) / math.sqrt(252.0) / haircut
float sigRth = na(sigDay) ? na : sigDay * math.sqrt(1.0 - onShare)

bool onOpen = anchorMode == "Today's open"

// WHICH close is "the prior close" depends on whether the bell has rung. `priorClose` only
// advances at an RTH open, so once the session ends it is a session STALE — on Friday evening
// it still holds THURSDAY's close, while `lastClose` holds Friday's. Pre-market is precisely
// when this panel gets read ("what is priced in for today?"), so reading the wrong one there
// would anchor the whole band a session behind. Inside RTH the roles swap: `lastClose` is
// being overwritten by the current bar, so only `priorClose` is stable.
float refClose = inRth ? priorClose : lastClose

float anchor  = onOpen ? dayOpen : refClose
float sigBand = onOpen ? sigRth : sigDay          // the pairing law, enforced in one line

float emPts = na(anchor) or na(sigBand) ? na : anchor * sigBand

float up1 = na(emPts) ? na : anchor + emPts
float dn1 = na(emPts) ? na : anchor - emPts
float up2 = na(emPts) ? na : anchor + 2.0 * emPts
float dn2 = na(emPts) ? na : anchor - 2.0 * emPts

// LEFT. Remaining variance is a share of the RTH slice only — the overnight slice is spent
// before the first bar of the session prints, whichever anchor the band uses.
float rem    = remainShare(u)
float remPts = na(sigRth) or na(rem) ? na : close * sigRth * math.sqrt(rem)

// Spent so far, measured against the SAME anchor the band uses. Mixing them (realised from the
// open, band from the prior close) is the quiet way to print a number that cannot be reconciled
// with the lines on the chart.
float movedPts = na(anchor) ? na : math.abs(close - anchor)
float usedPct  = na(movedPts) or na(emPts) or emPts == 0 ? na : movedPts / emPts * 100.0

// ─── Longer horizons ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Same machinery, tenor-matched index per horizon — but read the next paragraph before
// trusting the rationale, because the obvious one is WRONG.
//
// The tempting story is "each index at its native maturity calibrates best". MEASURED, that is
// false. backtest-expected-move.ts section 4b runs every index at every horizon and VIX's
// haircut is FLAT at ~1.35 across 1d / 5d / 21d over 9,221 sessions — it does not improve at
// its own 30-day tenor. The over-width is the variance risk premium baked into the LEVEL of the
// index, and sqrt(t) scaling carries that premium along with it. VIX1D simply has the smallest
// premium of the three, and in-sample it beat both at EVERY horizon (21-day haircut 0.98 vs
// VIX9D 1.12 vs VIX 1.19).
//
// The tenor-matched pairing is kept here because it is the market convention and it is what a
// chain quote would tell you — NOT because it measured best. On thin evidence (n=50
// non-overlapping months, one low-vol regime) VIX1D-scaled looked better. If you want the
// tighter empirical number, set the source manually and apply the measured haircut.
//
// Only SPX has a 9-day index. The other families own a single 30-day index, so on QQQ/IWM/DIA
// the WEEK row is that same 30-day number scaled down — the row names its source so a proxy is
// never mistaken for a measurement.
//
// Always anchored on the last CLOSE, never on "today's open": a month-ahead range measured from
// this morning's open is a category error, and mixing the two would make the rows unauditable
// against each other.
//
// MEASURED HAIRCUTS (common window, non-overlapping): WEEK via VIX9D = 1.22, MONTH via VIX =
// 1.19. Both bands run WIDE, same as DAY. Divide by those if you want a "what will probably
// happen" range rather than the risk-neutral one.
//
// MONDAY RUNS COLD, AND IT IS NOT A SCALING BUG. Splitting the 1-day result by weekday: Monday
// covers 81.4% against ~70% for Tue-Fri — haircut 1.29 vs ~1.08, so the band is ~20% wide on
// Mondays in std terms (~67% in variance terms).
//
// An earlier version of this comment blamed calendar-vs-trading-day annualisation. THAT WAS
// WRONG and the CBOE methodology above disproves it: VIX1D is business-time, weekends are zero
// minutes, and at Friday's close the 0DTE leg has expired so the index reads the NEXT-term
// (Monday) expiry alone at T = 405/102,060 = 1/252 — structurally identical to any ordinary
// overnight print. So (VIX1D_Fri/100)^2 x (1/252) IS exactly the option market's own implied
// variance for the Friday-close-to-Monday-close span. The formula is faithful; the overstatement
// lives entirely in OPTION PRICES.
//
// It is a real weekend variance risk premium, and two checks say so. (a) Friday's mean VIX1D is
// 16.66 — NOT marked down versus Wed 16.27 / Thu 17.05 — so market makers are not discounting
// the weekend, they are charging a full ordinary session's implied variance for a span that
// delivers far less. (b) French & Roll's true weekend excess is only +10.7% variance (1.052x in
// std), which is both the wrong size and, applied naively, the wrong SIGN — it would make Monday
// slightly too NARROW. Holiday weekends (4-day gaps, n=31) come in at haircut 1.109, near the
// ordinary-day baseline rather than more extreme, which a calendar-length mechanism cannot explain.
//
// CONSEQUENCE FOR A PREMIUM SELLER: this is an EDGE, not a defect to correct. Monday's implied is
// where the weekend premium sits. Do not haircut it away — and do not read a quiet Monday inside
// the band as information.
// These two are the same construction extended by sqrt(t), which assumes returns are iid —
// vol clustering and a term-varying risk premium both bend that over weeks. Treat WEEK and
// MONTH as well-founded estimates, not as the 72.6%-verified number DAY is.
int WK_DAYS = 5, MO_DAYS = 21

float idx30 = fam == "SPX" ? v30 : fam == "NDX" ? vxn : fam == "RUT" ? rvx
     : fam == "DJI" ? vxd : na
float idxWk = fam == "SPX" ? v9d : idx30
string wkName = fam == "SPX" ? "VIX9D" : fam == "NDX" ? "VXN" : fam == "RUT" ? "RVX"
     : fam == "DJI" ? "VXD" : "—"
string moName = fam == "SPX" ? "VIX" : wkName

hz(float ix, int days) =>
    na(ix) or na(refClose) ? na
         : refClose * (ix / 100.0) / math.sqrt(252.0) * math.sqrt(days) / haircut

float wkPts = hz(idxWk, WK_DAYS)
float moPts = hz(idx30, MO_DAYS)

rng(string nm, float pts) =>
    na(pts) or na(refClose) ? "—"
         : nm + " ±" + str.tostring(pts, format.mintick) + "   " +
           str.tostring(refClose - pts, format.mintick) + " · " +
           str.tostring(refClose + pts, format.mintick)

// ─── Plots ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// plot.style_linebr and an na outside RTH: style_line would bridge the overnight gap and draw
// a diagonal from yesterday's level to today's, which reads as a trend line that does not exist.
//
// plot() draws the LIVE session and nothing else. It cannot draw the others, because a plot
// value is committed on the bar that produced it and "am I inside the last N sessions?" is
// unknowable while an older bar is executing — the total session count does not exist yet.
// Prior sessions are line objects instead, drawn at barstate.islast where the count IS known.
// Today deliberately stays on plot(): that makes `Sessions drawn` = 1 a provable no-op against
// the version this replaced, rather than a re-implementation that merely looks the same.
color C_ANCH = #2e86de, C_1SIG = #e67e22, C_2SIG = #5c6879
color GO = #26a69a, WARN = #f0b429, STOP = #ef5350, MUTED = #5c6879

g(float v) => inRth and isLastSess ? v : na

plot(g(anchor), "Anchor", color = color.new(C_ANCH, 35), style = plot.style_linebr, linewidth = 1)
p1u = plot(g(up1), "+1σ", color = C_1SIG, style = plot.style_linebr, linewidth = 2)
p1d = plot(g(dn1), "−1σ", color = C_1SIG, style = plot.style_linebr, linewidth = 2)
plot(show2sd ? g(up2) : na, "+2σ", color = color.new(C_2SIG, 20), style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(show2sd ? g(dn2) : na, "−2σ", color = color.new(C_2SIG, 20), style = plot.style_linebr)

// fill() needs two plot handles to shade between, and it gets the VISIBLE ±1σ plots. An earlier
// version declared a second, hidden pair for this ("1σ upper (fill)" / "1σ lower (fill)",
// display.none). That worked, but display.none only hides a plot from the CHART — every named
// plot still appears in the Style tab forever, so the settings dialog listed two entries that
// drew nothing and could not be explained. Reusing the real plots deletes both rows.
//
// Transparency 100 rather than an na colour when the shading is off: identical on screen, and
// there is no na-colour edge case to reason about.
fill(p1u, p1d, color = color.new(C_1SIG, showZone ? 94 : 100), title = "1σ zone")

// ─── Prior sessions ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// One record per RTH session. Both numbers a band needs are FIXED for the whole session — the
// anchor is set at the open (or is yesterday's close) and the vol index is a prior daily close
// — so the record is written once and only its right edge advances.
type Sess
    int   startIdx
    int   endIdx
    int   startMs
    float anchor
    float em

var array<Sess> hist = array.new<Sess>()
var bool recorded = false

if rthOpenBar
    recorded := false

// Gated on na, not merely on inRth. The first session on any chart has no prior close, so its
// anchor never resolves — pushing it would hand line.new an na price. Writing on the first
// VALID bar rather than unconditionally at the open also covers a vol index that arrives late:
// the band starts where it became real instead of not existing at all.
if inRth and not na(anchor) and not na(emPts)
    if not recorded
        array.push(hist, Sess.new(bar_index, bar_index, sessStart, anchor, emPts))
        recorded := true
    else
        Sess cur = array.last(hist)
        cur.endIdx := bar_index

var array<line> drawn = array.new<line>()
var array<box>  zones = array.new<box>()

// Not named hline() — that is a Pine built-in, and shadowing it is a fight you cannot win.
bandLine(int x1, int x2, float y, color c) =>
    array.push(drawn, line.new(x1, y, x2, y, color = c, width = 1))

if barstate.islast
    // Cleared UNCONDITIONALLY, before anything decides what to draw. Clear inside the draw
    // branch instead and a user who sets 5 and then goes back to 1 keeps four ghost sessions
    // hanging on the chart until the study is reloaded.
    for ln in drawn
        line.delete(ln)
    array.clear(drawn)
    for bx in zones
        box.delete(bx)
    array.clear(zones)

    int n = array.size(hist)
    // The n > 0 guard is not defensive noise: `for i = 0 to -1` counts DOWN in Pine and would
    // read index -1 out of an empty array. Ceiling arithmetic against the input's maxval of 30
    // and max_lines_count above: 29 prior sessions × 5 lines = 145 lines, 29 boxes.
    //
    // The loop runs even at histSess = 1, and that is load-bearing. PRE-MARKET, today has no
    // RTH bars yet, so plot() has nothing to draw and the skip below matches no record — the
    // one session in the window is YESTERDAY, and it gets drawn here. Gating this on
    // histSess > 1 instead would blank the chart entirely between the cash close and the next
    // open, which is exactly when this panel gets read.
    if n > 0
        // A ternary and not math.max(): the loop bound must be an int, and math.max is the
        // float overload the moment anything about its arguments is ambiguous.
        int first = n > histSess ? n - histSess : 0
        for i = first to n - 1
            Sess s = array.get(hist, i)
            // The live session belongs to plot(). Drawing it here too would double the ink.
            if s.startMs != lastSessStart
                float u1 = s.anchor + s.em
                float d1 = s.anchor - s.em
                if showZone
                    array.push(zones, box.new(s.startIdx, u1, s.endIdx, d1,
                         border_width = 0, bgcolor = color.new(C_1SIG, 96)))
                bandLine(s.startIdx, s.endIdx, s.anchor, color.new(C_ANCH, 70))
                bandLine(s.startIdx, s.endIdx, u1, color.new(C_1SIG, 55))
                bandLine(s.startIdx, s.endIdx, d1, color.new(C_1SIG, 55))
                if show2sd
                    bandLine(s.startIdx, s.endIdx, s.anchor + 2.0 * s.em, color.new(C_2SIG, 60))
                    bandLine(s.startIdx, s.endIdx, s.anchor - 2.0 * s.em, color.new(C_2SIG, 60))

// ─── Right-edge labels ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The band levels carry their own price, so they stay readable with the panel off.
var label lU1 = na, var label lD1 = na, var label lU2 = na, var label lD2 = na

tag(label old, float px, string txt, color c) =>
    label.delete(old)
    na(px) ? na : label.new(bar_index + 3, px, txt, style = label.style_label_left,
         color = #0f1521, textcolor = c, size = size.small)

if barstate.islast
    lU1 := tag(lU1, up1, "+1σ  " + str.tostring(up1, format.mintick), C_1SIG)
    lD1 := tag(lD1, dn1, "−1σ  " + str.tostring(dn1, format.mintick), C_1SIG)
    if show2sd
        lU2 := tag(lU2, up2, "+2σ  " + str.tostring(up2, format.mintick), C_2SIG)
        lD2 := tag(lD2, dn2, "−2σ  " + str.tostring(dn2, format.mintick), C_2SIG)
    else
        label.delete(lU2)
        label.delete(lD2)

// ─── Readout ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// LEFT sits at the top because it is the live question. Every row below it is an audit trail
// for that one number — a verdict you cannot reconstruct from the same panel is a black box.
//
// A missing feed must never render as a small, calm expected move. Every cell falls back to an
// em-dash and the source row turns red, rather than quietly showing a stale or zero band.
var table t = table.new(position.top_right, 2, 8, border_width = 0,
     frame_width = 1, frame_color = #27313f)

pct(float v) => na(v) ? "—" : str.tostring(v, "#.##") + "%"

if barstate.islast
    if showTbl
        bool live = not na(idx) and not na(emPts)

        table.cell(t, 0, 0, "LEFT", text_color = #9aa3b8, text_size = size.small)
        table.cell(t, 1, 0, na(remPts) ? (inRth ? "—" : "closed")
             : "±" + str.tostring(remPts, format.mintick) + "   " +
               pct(na(close) or close == 0 ? na : remPts / close * 100.0),
             text_color = na(remPts) ? MUTED : GO, text_size = size.large)

        table.cell(t, 0, 1, "DAY", text_color = #9aa3b8, text_size = size.small)
        table.cell(t, 1, 1, na(emPts) ? "—"
             : "±" + str.tostring(emPts, format.mintick) + "   " +
               pct(na(sigBand) ? na : sigBand * 100.0),
             text_color = #d6dbe4, text_size = size.normal)

        table.cell(t, 0, 2, "USED", text_color = #9aa3b8, text_size = size.small)
        table.cell(t, 1, 2, na(usedPct) ? "—"
             : str.tostring(usedPct, "#") + "%  of day   (" +
               str.tostring(movedPts, format.mintick) + ")",
             text_color = na(usedPct) ? MUTED
                  : usedPct >= 100 ? STOP : usedPct >= 70 ? WARN : #d6dbe4,
             text_size = size.small)

        table.cell(t, 0, 3, "BAND", text_color = #9aa3b8, text_size = size.small)
        table.cell(t, 1, 3, na(emPts) ? "—"
             : str.tostring(dn1, format.mintick) + "  ·  " + str.tostring(up1, format.mintick),
             text_color = #d6dbe4, text_size = size.small)

        table.cell(t, 0, 4, "WEEK", text_color = #9aa3b8, text_size = size.small)
        table.cell(t, 1, 4, rng(wkName, wkPts), text_color = #9aa3b8, text_size = size.small)

        table.cell(t, 0, 5, "MONTH", text_color = #9aa3b8, text_size = size.small)
        table.cell(t, 1, 5, rng(moName, moPts), text_color = #9aa3b8, text_size = size.small)

        table.cell(t, 0, 6, "SESS", text_color = #9aa3b8, text_size = size.small)
        table.cell(t, 1, 6, na(u) ? "pre/post"
             : str.tostring(u * 100.0, "#") + "% elapsed · " +
               str.tostring(rem * 100.0, "#") + "% var left",
             text_color = #d6dbe4, text_size = size.small)

        // The source row is the honesty row: which index, its level, whether the horizon
        // actually matches a single session, and whether the index even measures this chart.
        // An unmappable symbol says so by NAME — "no vol index" alone would leave the reader
        // guessing whether the feed died or the mapping missed.
        string horizon = useSrc == "VIX1D" ? "1-day implied"
             : useSrc == "VIX9D" ? "9-day proxy" : "30-day proxy"
        table.cell(t, 0, 7, "SRC", text_color = #9aa3b8, text_size = size.small)
        table.cell(t, 1, 7, useSrc == "NONE" ? "no vol index for " + syminfo.ticker
             : not live ? useSrc + "  NO DATA"
             : useSrc + " " + str.tostring(idx, "#.##") + "  " + horizon +
               (mismatch ? "  ≠ " + fam : "") +
               (haircut > 1.0 ? "  ÷" + str.tostring(haircut, "#.##") : ""),
             text_color = (useSrc == "NONE" or not live) ? STOP
                  : (mismatch or useSrc != "VIX1D") ? WARN : #d6dbe4,
             text_size = size.small)
    else
        table.clear(t, 0, 0, 1, 7)

// ─── Alerts ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Transition-fired, not level-fired — a level-triggered alert on "outside the band" repeats
// every bar for as long as price stays there.
bool out1 = inRth and not na(up1) and (close > up1 or close < dn1)
bool out2 = inRth and not na(up2) and (close > up2 or close < dn2)

alertcondition(out1 and not out1[1], "Expected Move · 1σ breached",
     "SPX outside the daily 1σ expected-move band.")
alertcondition(out2 and not out2[1], "Expected Move · 2σ breached",
     "SPX outside the daily 2σ expected-move band — a ~5% tail day.")
